mcp__<name>__get_tool_schema + mcp__<name>__invoke_tool); their server‑side
source is not in any local workspace. The actionable local authoring
convention therefore comes from a fully‑working FastMCP server already in this
repo: src/openteam/mcp_server/ (server.py, context.py, _helpers.py, cli.py,
templates/SKILL.md, templates/mcp.json — 336 lines total) plus the per‑tool
descriptor pattern in src/openteam/server/resources/tools/<tool>/tool.json,
the tests in test/openteam/mcp_server/, and the "skill wraps a CLI" pattern in
~/.agents/skills/twg/SKILL.md.

Negative evidence (critical): zero mlflow, tecton, or databricks imports
anywhere in responsible-ai-api/src/; ml_platform/ only contains
input_moderation_client.py and use_cases.py. The only reusable wrapper pattern
present is slauth (10 callsites in refactored/). The three ML‑lifecycle tools
must therefore be authored as net‑new wrappers — but the MCP scaffolding is
fully templatable from OpenTeam.

Hard rules extracted (each non‑negotiable, traced to file):

  1 One async async def openteam_<tool>(typed kwargs) -> str per MCP tool,
    registered via _WRAPPERS dict and
    mcp.add_tool(FunctionTool.from_function(...)).
  2 Lazy executor import inside the wrapper body — the test pattern
    (test_wrappers_smoke.py) monkeypatches the executor module and requires
    this.
  3 strip_unset must preserve the explicit 4‑clause form (v is not None and v
    is not False and v != "" and v != []) — collapsing to v in (None, False, 
    "", []) drops integer 0 (real ML metric/step values).
  4 Mutually exclusive flags collapse to a single Literal[...] enum at the MCP
    surface (the SKILL.md flags this as a "common pitfall").
  5 render_result() is the single output sink and auto‑surfaces any
    context_updates keys ending in _path/_dir as an Artifacts: footer — use
    this for replay reports, audit CSVs.
  6 295 s hard MCP timeout is the documented limit; long jobs either split
    into submit + poll (socrates pattern) or route to the slash surface.
  7 Auth lives in context.py::_ENV_MAP, not in wrapper signatures — extend
    with DATABRICKS_HOST/TOKEN, MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI, TECTON_API_KEY/URL,
    UNITY_CATALOG_METASTORE, SLAUTH_ASAP_KEY.
  8 Errors fail‑fast at the wrapper boundary (re‑raise + log, never swallow);
    retries live in the SDK layer in the executor.
  9 Selective tool registration via create_<server>_server(tool_names: 
    list[str] | None) is contractually tested (test_server_factory.py).
 10 Writes get confirm: bool = False per the TWG mutation‑safety rule.

Cross‑server composition patterns to copy:

 • Submit → poll → fetch from socrates (create_query/get_query_status) → apply
   to MLflow metric replay (KPI 8.1) and Databricks Jobs.
 • Search → get from compass → apply to MLflow registry browsing and UC table
   discovery.
 • Compressed action enum from bitbucket (bitbucketRepository(action=…)) →
   apply to MLflow registry CRUD bundle.
 • Federated projection emitting CSV artifact from TWG (work query, org-tree)
   → directly applicable to the parent‑task connector/region/tenant audit and
   reproducibility audit.

Recommended package layout for the implementation: one umbrella
mle/mcp_server/ FastMCP server (copy of OpenTeam's), per‑tool tool.json
descriptors under mle/server/resources/tools/<tool>/, mirrored tests under
test/mle/mcp_server/, plus shipped templates/SKILL.md (with allowed-tools:
listing all ~13 wrappers) and templates/mcp.json snippet. Three‑server‑family
option also viable (mle-mlflow, mle-feature-store, mle-databricks), but the
local convention (selective --tools flag on one server) favours the umbrella.

Open questions for cross‑facet synthesis: server boundary (one vs three),
Slauth‑vs‑PAT precedence (role‑doc §4.3 not in scope here), bit‑exact‑replay
algorithm (the convention here is the delivery mechanism only), and the actual
Tecton metadata query for the audit projection.


