# Research Findings: Symphony3-Tier Autonomy Model & Non-Negotiable Manifest Schema Fields

## 1. The Symphony 3-Tier Autonomy Model

### What It Is
The Symphony 3-Tier Autonomy Model is Atlassian's trust framework governing how AI employees operate within the Symphony orchestration platform. It defines three graduated tiers of agent autonomy, expressed as role-specific policies that determine when agents act independently, seek confirmation, or escalate entirely to humans.

### How It Works

| **Tier** | **Signal** | **Meaning** | **Criteria** |
|----------|-----------|-------------|--------------|
| **Tier 1— Autonomous** | 🟢 | Agent acts without human approval | Known pattern, within policy scope, bounded blast radius |
| **Tier 2 — Confirm** | 🟡 | Agent proposes action with reasoning; human approves with single click | Novel patterns or higher-stakes decisions |
| **Tier 3 — Escalate** | 🔴 | Agent hits a hard policy boundary; pages human with full context and reasoning trace | Human makes the call entirely |

**Key Design Principles:**
- Policies are **version-controlled**, **auditable**, and **human-authored**
- The AI employee proposes policy updates when it identifies gaps — humans approve ALL policy changes
- Symphony's autonomy is **bounded by what humans have explicitly permitted** — it never exceeds it
- Reasoning traces are captured throughout every agent execution

### Source
- [Atlassian Symphony: AI Employee Orchestration Platform](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6933824809)
- [AI Employee Strategy](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6742955831)

### How It Applies to SKILL.md
In a SKILL.md file, each workflow step should be annotated with its autonomy tier classification. The skill should include:
1. A decision tree or lookup table mapping action types to tiers
2. Clear escalation instructions when the agent cannot determine the appropriate tier
3. Reasoning-trace capture requirements at each step

---

## 2. Role-Specific Autonomy Tier Application (MLE Agent Context)

### What It Is
The3-tier model is applied differently per role. For the Digital MLE, the tiers map to experiment lifecycle activities:

### How It Works (Derived from Digital TPM Model + AI Employee Strategy)

| **Action Category** | **Tier** | **Rationale** |
|---------------------|----------|---------------|
| Running pre-approved experiment variants | 🟢 Autonomous | Known pattern, bounded resource usage |
| Data quality monitoring & alerting | 🟢 Autonomous | Routine, bounded blast radius |
| Filing experiment results and summaries | 🟢 Autonomous | Non-destructive, information-only |
| Triggering retraining on drift detection | 🟡 Confirm | Novel resource commitment, potential production impact |
| Proposing hyperparameter changes | 🟡 Confirm | Higher-stakes decision |
| Model promotion to production | 🔴 Escalate | Hard policy boundary — human must decide |
| Rollback decisions | 🔴 Escalate | Critical customer impact potential |
| Architecture/design changes | 🔴 Escalate | Novel, high blast radius |

### Source
- [Digital TPM: AI Employee Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6900978790) — demonstrates the 3-tier model applied per responsibility area
- [AIF (PAI) L3-O1-KR1: AI Employee](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/aifun/pages/6978208776) — defines MLE agent KR scoring

### How It Applies to SKILL.md
Each skill should include a **Tier Classification Matrix** that maps every MCP operation or tool call to its default autonomy tier. The matrix should note conditions under which a tier escalates (e.g., a normally-🟢 action becomes🟡 if the model is in production or if resource usage exceeds a threshold).

