# Research: Drift Detection Signals, Retraining Triggers, and 🟢/🟡/🔴 Autonomy Thresholds for SOP-1

## Executive Summary

This research synthesizes findings from Atlassian's internal ML Platform documentation, the AI Employee Strategy framework, and industry-standard drift detection methodologies to define the drift detection signals, retraining triggers, and autonomy tier classification for SOP-1 (Drift-Triggered Retraining). The findings are directly actionable for encoding into a SKILL.md workflow.

---

## 1. Drift Detection Signals

### 1.1 Signal Categories (from Atlassian ML Platform)

Based on the internal documentation across ML Lens, Feature Drift Detection Architecture, and CoreML MLOps Enhancement Plan, drift signals fall into **four categories**:

| **Signal Category** | **What It Detects** | **Tools/Platform** | **Metrics** |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Data Quality Drift** | Input data pipeline degradation (nulls, schema changes, volume drops) | ML Lens Data Quality SDK | null_pct, row_count_change, zero_pct, schema_shape_expected |
| **Feature Distribution Drift** | Statistical shift in feature distributions vs. reference | ML Lens + StarRocks / ML Studio | PSI, KS test, JSD, Chi-squared, Wasserstein |
| **Output/Prediction Drift** | Shift in model prediction distributions | ML Lens metric store | PSI on prediction distribution, KS on scores |
| **Model Performance Drift** | Degradation in evaluation metrics | ML Lens + offline eval pipelines | NDCG, MRR, MAP, precision@k, recall@k, F1 |

**Source**: [Design: ML Lens Platform Data Drift Detection](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6738511349), [CoreML MLOps Enhancement Plan](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~549416031/pages/6485707740), [H2 Action Plan: Model Observability](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6495897543)

### 1.2 Statistical Tests by Feature Type

From the internal Feature Drift Detection Architecture Proposal and CoreML documents:

| **Feature Type** | **Primary Test** | **What It Measures** | **Threshold Interpretation** |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Continuous (numeric)** | Population Stability Index (PSI) | Divergence of current distribution from reference | PSI < 0.1 = no drift;0.1–0.25 = moderate; > 0.25 = significant |
| **Continuous (numeric)** | Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) Test | Maximum difference between two CDFs | p-value < 0.05 = significant drift |
| **Continuous (numeric)** | Wasserstein Distance | Earth-mover distance between distributions | Captures magnitude of shift (no standard threshold — model-specific) |
| **Categorical** | Chi-squared Test | Whether category proportions have changed | p-value < 0.05 = significant drift |
| **Categorical** | Jensen-Shannon Divergence (JSD) | Symmetric divergence measure | 0 = identical, 1 = maximally different; >0.1 typical alert threshold |

**Source**: [CoreML MLOps Enhancement Plan §4.3.1](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~549416031/pages/6485707740), [Feature Drift Detection Architecture Proposal](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/KYC/pages/6866709029)

### 1.3 Reference Window Strategy

From the ML Lens Platform Data Drift Detection design:

| **Window Type** | **Duration** | **Use Case** | **When to Use** |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Training baseline** | Point-in-time at model training | Long-term structural drift (have features fundamentally changed?) | Always — primary comparison |
| **Rolling28-day** | Trailing 28 days of production data | Short-term operational drift (recent pipeline break?) | Default for ML Lens platform |
| **Same-period prior year** | Seasonal comparison window | Seasonality-adjusted drift (expected holiday pattern?) | Models with known cyclical patterns |
| **Rolling 30-day** | Trailing 30 days | Alternative short-term window | Per CoreML documentation |

**Key implementation detail**: The reference and current windows need NOT be equal in size. The ML Lens design uses a 28-day reference with a 1-day current window. Drift metrics (PSI, KS) compare normalized distributions, not raw counts.

**Source**: [Design: ML Lens Platform Data Drift Detection](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6738511349)

### 1.4 Weighted Drift Score (Multi-Feature Aggregation)

From the H2 Action Plan for CoreML:

```
weighted_drift_score = Σ (PSI_i × normalized_shap_importance_i)
```

This provides a single aggregate signal that weights feature drift by feature importance (SHAP values), preventing low-importance features from triggering false alarms.

**Source**: [H2 Action Plan: Model Observability & Performance](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6495897543)

### 1.5 Two-Stage Alerting (Input → Output Correlation)

The H2 Action Plan documents a sophisticated two-stage system:

**Stage 1**: Feature Drift Detection → compute weighted_drift_score
**Stage 2**: If threshold exceeded → compute output distribution shift → confirm or dismiss

| **Input Drift** | **Output Drift** | **Severity** | **Action** |
|---|---|---|---|
| High | High | P0 | Likely model degradation. Investigate immediately. **Consider model retraining.** |
| High | Low | P1 | Model appears robust. Monitor closely. Feature may have compensating effects. |
| Low | High | P0 | Unexpected output driver. Could be concept drift or label shift. |
| Low | Low | Normal | No action required. |

**Source**: [H2 Action Plan: Model Observability & Performance §4.3.3](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6495897543)

---

## 2. Retraining Triggers

### 2.1 Atlassian's Automated Retraining Architecture

From the RFC on Model Re-training using Data Quality and ML Lens:

**Architecture Flow**:
1. ML Lens SDK computes metrics during data processing jobs (daily/scheduled)
2. ML Lens emits metric events + expectations to **StreamHub** event bus
3. **ML Studio Orchestrator** consumes events from StreamHub (via SQS)
4. Orchestrator evaluates user-defined **retraining conditions**
5. If conditions met → programmatically triggers retraining workflow

**Key Design Decision**: Thresholds are user-defined per model/use-case. The platform does not impose default retraining thresholds (this is an open question in the RFC).

**Source**: [RFC: Model Re-training using Data Quality and ML Lens](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6013257858)

### 2.2 Trigger Types

From multiple Atlassian sources:

| **Trigger Type** | **Signal Source** | **Implementation Status** | **Autonomy Tier** |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Scheduled (CRON-based)** | Time-based cadence (e.g., 30 days) | ✅ Available in ML Studio | 🟢 Autonomous |
| **Data quality breach** | ML Lens Data Quality SDK (null counts, row counts, schema) | 🔨 In progress (RFC approved) | 🟢 Autonomous (for monitoring) → 🟡 Confirm (for trigger) |
| **Feature drift threshold breach** | ML Lens drift metrics (PSI > threshold) | 🔨 In progress (future extension) | 🟡 Confirm |
| **Model performance degradation** | Offline eval metrics (NDCG, MRR) below gate |📋 Planned (future extension) | 🟡 Confirm |
| **Data freshness SLA breach** | Model age exceeds refresh policy window | Described in Digital MLE Vision | 🟢 Autonomous |
| **On-demand (manual/ad-hoc)** | Human-triggered |✅ Available |🔴 Human-initiated |

**Source**: [RFC: Model Re-training](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6013257858), [ML Studio Continuous Training Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/4285958106), [Digital MLE Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/7051610727)

### 2.3 PSI Threshold Levels for Retraining Decisions

From CoreML documentation and real-world EWS investigation:

| **PSI Range** | **Severity** | **Interpretation** | **Recommended Action** |
|---|---|---|---|
| PSI < 0.1 | No drift | Distributions are essentially the same | No action |
| 0.1 ≤ PSI < 0.25 | Moderate drift | Notable shift; investigate root cause | Monitor closely, prepare for retraining |
| PSI ≥ 0.25 | Significant drift | Major distributional change | **Trigger retraining workflow** |
| PSI > 0.5 | Strong/severe drift | Extreme shift (confirmed in EWS: PSI=0.54 for AI usage features) | Immediate retraining + root cause analysis |

**Real-world validation**: The EWS Internal Investigation confirmed PSI=0.54 for AI usage features between 2024→2025 training/inference windows, classified as "Strong Drift" and directly linked to model performance degradation.

**Source**: [CoreML MLOps Enhancement Plan §4.3.1](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~549416031/pages/6485707740), [EWS Internal Investigation](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020a2953c1f5bf3459f9bbaba57e976cd23/pages/6731644693)

### 2.4 Data Quality SDK Threshold (OutputDistributionShift)

From the Quality SDK Architecture document:

```python
{"column": "predict_m2_purchase", "psi": 0.03, "threshold": 0.2}
# Passes when: psi <= psi_threshold (default 0.2)
```

The Quality SDK's `OutputDistributionShift` check uses PSI with a default threshold of **0.2** for pass/fail determination.

**Source**: [Quality SDK: Architecture & Check Reference](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~549416031/pages/6520868784)

### 2.5 ML Lens Alert Configuration Pattern

From the ML Lens alerting documentation:

```json
[
  {
    "metric_name": "exact_match_rate",
    "threshold": 80.0,
    "comparison": "lt",
    "alert_name": "Low Exact Match Rate",
    "slack_channel": "#ml-lens-alerts-test"
  },
  {
    "metric_name": "null_rate_diff",
    "threshold": 15.0,
    "comparison": "gt",
    "alert_name": "High Null Rate Diff",
    "jsm_priority": "P2",
    "jsm_responder_id": "team-id",
    "jsm_responder_type": "TEAM"
  }
]
```

**Source**: [GUIDE: MLFS Migration Feature Observability](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6764547543)

### 2.6 Drift Metrics Table Schema (for persistence)

From the CoreML H2 Action Plan — the standard schema for persisting drift signals:

```
core_ml_data.coreml_drift_metrics
├── ds (STRING)                -- date stamp
├── pipeline_name (STRING)                -- pipeline identifier
├── model_name (STRING)                   -- model identifier
├── feature_name (STRING)                 -- NULL for output-level drift
├── drift_test (STRING)                   -- "psi", "ks", "jsd", "chi2", "wasserstein"
├── drift_value (DOUBLE)                  -- computed metric value
├── drift_threshold_warning (DOUBLE)      -- warning threshold
├── drift_threshold_error (DOUBLE)        -- error/retraining threshold
├── reference_window (STRING)             -- "training", "30d_rolling", "yoy"
├── feature_importance_tier (STRING)      -- high/medium/low
├── status (STRING)                       -- ok/warning/error
```

**Source**: [H2 Action Plan: Model Observability §4.3.4](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6495897543)

