## 1. Drift Detection Signals

### 1.1 Signal Categories (from Atlassian ML Platform)

Based on the internal documentation across ML Lens, Feature Drift Detection Architecture, and CoreML MLOps Enhancement Plan, drift signals fall into **four categories**:

| **Signal Category** | **What It Detects** | **Tools/Platform** | **Metrics** |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Data Quality Drift** | Input data pipeline degradation (nulls, schema changes, volume drops) | ML Lens Data Quality SDK | null_pct, row_count_change, zero_pct, schema_shape_expected |
| **Feature Distribution Drift** | Statistical shift in feature distributions vs. reference | ML Lens + StarRocks / ML Studio | PSI, KS test, JSD, Chi-squared, Wasserstein |
| **Output/Prediction Drift** | Shift in model prediction distributions | ML Lens metric store | PSI on prediction distribution, KS on scores |
| **Model Performance Drift** | Degradation in evaluation metrics | ML Lens + offline eval pipelines | NDCG, MRR, MAP, precision@k, recall@k, F1 |

**Source**: [Design: ML Lens Platform Data Drift Detection](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6738511349), [CoreML MLOps Enhancement Plan](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~549416031/pages/6485707740), [H2 Action Plan: Model Observability](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6495897543)

### 1.2 Statistical Tests by Feature Type

From the internal Feature Drift Detection Architecture Proposal and CoreML documents:

| **Feature Type** | **Primary Test** | **What It Measures** | **Threshold Interpretation** |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Continuous (numeric)** | Population Stability Index (PSI) | Divergence of current distribution from reference | PSI < 0.1 = no drift;0.1–0.25 = moderate; > 0.25 = significant |
| **Continuous (numeric)** | Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) Test | Maximum difference between two CDFs | p-value < 0.05 = significant drift |
| **Continuous (numeric)** | Wasserstein Distance | Earth-mover distance between distributions | Captures magnitude of shift (no standard threshold — model-specific) |
| **Categorical** | Chi-squared Test | Whether category proportions have changed | p-value < 0.05 = significant drift |
| **Categorical** | Jensen-Shannon Divergence (JSD) | Symmetric divergence measure | 0 = identical, 1 = maximally different; >0.1 typical alert threshold |

**Source**: [CoreML MLOps Enhancement Plan §4.3.1](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~549416031/pages/6485707740), [Feature Drift Detection Architecture Proposal](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/KYC/pages/6866709029)

### 1.3 Reference Window Strategy

From the ML Lens Platform Data Drift Detection design:

| **Window Type** | **Duration** | **Use Case** | **When to Use** |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Training baseline** | Point-in-time at model training | Long-term structural drift (have features fundamentally changed?) | Always — primary comparison |
| **Rolling28-day** | Trailing 28 days of production data | Short-term operational drift (recent pipeline break?) | Default for ML Lens platform |
| **Same-period prior year** | Seasonal comparison window | Seasonality-adjusted drift (expected holiday pattern?) | Models with known cyclical patterns |
| **Rolling 30-day** | Trailing 30 days | Alternative short-term window | Per CoreML documentation |

**Key implementation detail**: The reference and current windows need NOT be equal in size. The ML Lens design uses a 28-day reference with a 1-day current window. Drift metrics (PSI, KS) compare normalized distributions, not raw counts.

**Source**: [Design: ML Lens Platform Data Drift Detection](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6738511349)

### 1.4 Weighted Drift Score (Multi-Feature Aggregation)

From the H2 Action Plan for CoreML:

```
weighted_drift_score = Σ (PSI_i × normalized_shap_importance_i)
```

This provides a single aggregate signal that weights feature drift by feature importance (SHAP values), preventing low-importance features from triggering false alarms.

**Source**: [H2 Action Plan: Model Observability & Performance](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6495897543)

### 1.5 Two-Stage Alerting (Input → Output Correlation)

The H2 Action Plan documents a sophisticated two-stage system:

**Stage 1**: Feature Drift Detection → compute weighted_drift_score
**Stage 2**: If threshold exceeded → compute output distribution shift → confirm or dismiss

| **Input Drift** | **Output Drift** | **Severity** | **Action** |
|---|---|---|---|
| High | High | P0 | Likely model degradation. Investigate immediately. **Consider model retraining.** |
| High | Low | P1 | Model appears robust. Monitor closely. Feature may have compensating effects. |
| Low | High | P0 | Unexpected output driver. Could be concept drift or label shift. |
| Low | Low | Normal | No action required. |

**Source**: [H2 Action Plan: Model Observability & Performance §4.3.3](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6495897543)

