## 4. Existing Platform Tools and Integration Points

### 4.1 ML Lens Integration (Drift Detection)

**Drift Detection Cycle** (from MLFS Feature Observability diagrams):
1. MLFS logs feature values → ML Lens API
2. ML Lens validates schema, flattens nested values
3. Data written to StarRocks (feature_logs table)
4. Every ~5 minutes, Drift Scheduler triggers computation
5. Drift Calculator runs SQL aggregation queries
6. Computes PSI, KS, null%, skewness
7. Writes results to feature_metrics table
8. Emits metric events to Alert Engine
9. Alert Engine evaluates thresholds (platform defaults + custom)
10. If threshold breached → Trigger alert (Slack/PagerDuty/JSM)

**Source**: [MLFS Feature Observability Design Option Diagrams](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6949454822)

### 4.2 ML Studio Orchestrator (Retraining Trigger)

- Consumes events from StreamHub via SQS
- Evaluates user-defined retraining conditions
- Programmatically triggers workflows via `/run` API
- Supports scheduled (CRON) and event-driven triggers

### 4.3 Key Platforms Referenced

| **Platform** | **Role in SOP-1** | **MCP Integration** |
|---|---|---|
| ML Lens | Drift metrics computation and alerting | eval-framework (new tool) |
| ML Studio | Training workflow orchestration and execution | ML CI/CD (new tool) |
| ML Registry | Model versioning and tagging | ML CI/CD (new tool) |
| StreamHub | Event bus for metric-to-orchestrator communication | Internal (not MCP-exposed) |
| Slack | Alert notifications (30-min cadence in SOP-3) | slack MCP |
| Confluence | Drift report documentation | atlassian MCP |
| Jira | Retraining work item tracking | atlassian MCP |
| Bitbucket | Model code/config version control | bitbucket MCP |

