# SOP-2Best Practices Research: New Experiment Cycle

## Research Findings for ML Experiment Cycle SKILL.md

This document provides actionable research findings for authoring the SOP-2 (New Experiment Cycle) SKILL.md, covering hypothesis framing, literature synthesis patterns, parallel-architecture orchestration, ablation standards, error-analysis frameworks, and decision criteria.

---

## 1. HYPOTHESIS FRAMING

### 1.1 What It Is
A structured method for stating testable ML experiment hypotheses that clearly define the causal relationship, directionality, measurable variables, and falsifiability criteria.

### 1.2 How It Works — Atlassian-Internal Template

From internal Experimentation Best Practices ([Hypothesis-driven design](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/band/pages/3666042907/Hypothesis-driven+design+-+what+why+and+how)):

**Template:**
```
We believe that: [assumption/change] will lead to: [measurable outcome]
We will test this by doing: [plan/experiment design]
We expect to see: [quantitative threshold/expected result]
```

**Rules (from internal docs):**
1. States a causal relationship with a clear actor
2. Has directionality (increase/decrease)
3. States variables that can be manipulated AND measured
4. Is testable with an experiment
5. Is a falsifiable statement

**Growth Engineering Framework extension** (from [Experiment Design Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/Growth/pages/3110851265/Experiment+Design+JPD-ID)):
```
Hypothesis:IF [we make this specific change]
  THEN [this metric will improve by X%]
  BECAUSE [mechanism/rationale]
  We will succeed if: we see a [Y]% uplift in [primary metric]
```

### 1.3 ML-Specific Hypothesis Framing (Synthesized from Multiple Sources)

For ML experiments, the hypothesis must additionally specify:

| Field | Description | Example |
|-------|-------------|---------|
| **Assumption** | What you believe about the model/data | "Adding behavioral features improves relevance for tenants with >1K issues" |
| **Independent Variable** | What you're changing | Model architecture, hyperparameter, feature set, data preprocessing |
| **Dependent Variable** | What you're measuring | MRR@10, NDCG@10, latency P90, inference cost |
| **Control** | Baseline comparison | Current production model / previous best experiment |
| **Mechanism** | Why you believe this will work | "Behavioral signals capture user intent not represented in content features" |
| **Kill criteria** | When to abandon | "If offline metrics regress >2% on any guardrail metric" |
| **Expected magnitude** | How much improvement | "+5% MRR@10 relative lift" |

### 1.4 How It Applies to SKILL.md

The AI employee should:
- **🟢 Autonomous**: Auto-generate hypothesis template with placeholders filled from prior experiment context
- **🟡 Confirm**: Validate hypothesis completeness (all fields present) and flag missing kill criteria
- **🔴 Human required**: Approve the hypothesis statement and expected magnitude before experiment execution begins

**Source:** [Experimentation Best Practices - Hypothesis Driven - Impact Phase1 - J-LOVE](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/JLKB/pages/1880472371), [Hypothesis-driven design](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/band/pages/3666042907), [Experiment Design Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/Growth/pages/3110851265), [ML-driven MFY Experiment Phase Checklist](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/Spork/pages/6157225057)

---

## 2. LITERATURE SYNTHESIS PATTERN

### 2.1 What It Is
A systematic workflow for reviewing and synthesizing prior work (academic papers, internal experiments, and external benchmarks) before starting a new experiment.

### 2.2 How It Works — Internal Patterns

**Weekly ML Research Digest Pattern** (from [Weekly ML Research Digest —2026-04-26](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020d99ef610963a4c71971dd69cb6705ca0/pages/6889646944)):
```
Sources fetched: ArXiv cs.IR (direct API), ArXiv cs.CL (direct API), HuggingFace, GitHub Trending
Verification: Every ArXiv ID independently verified via direct API call
Structure:
  - TL;DR (5 bullets)
  - Worth Reading This Week (top 3 with "Why it matters for [team]")
  - Cross-cutting signals (patterns relevant to current work)
  - Full catalog with per-paper summaries
```

**Literature Review Agent Pipeline** (from [Literature Review Agent — Setup Guide](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020da27561b9bb14249879a9abe6a41ad1c/pages/6698141159)):
```
Stage 1: Research_Synthesizer → "State of the Research"│Reads all sources, extracts direct quotes, classifies evidence↓
Stage 2: Integrity_Auditor → Integrity Audit Report
    │   Independently verifies every quote, checks statuses,
    │   catches hallucinations, validates search completeness
    ↓
QUALITY GATE: If Quote Accuracy< 80%, report flagged as unreliable
    ↓
Stage 3: Insight_Critic → Gap Analysis & Critique
    │   Finds contradictions, sample bias, assigns Research
    │   Maturity Scores per theme, identifies what's missing
    ↓
[Final: Verified Literature Review with confidence ratings]
```

**CoreML Research Paper Reading Club Pattern** (from [CoreML Research Paper Reading Session](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/5653482073)):
- Organized by category: Surveys/Overviews → Foundational Papers → Application Papers
- Each paper entry has: Link, Contribution, and "Why it matters" annotation
- Cross-paper synthesis into concrete actionable principles

### 2.3 Synthesis Output Structure for ML Experiments

Based on the [Academic Literature Review —5 ArXiv Papers](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~111538891/pages/7035099887) pattern:

```markdown
## Literature Synthesis for Experiment: [NAME]

### Prior Internal Experiments
| Experiment | Result | Key Learning | Applies Because |
|-----------|--------|-------------|-----------------|

### External Research (ArXiv, conferences)
| Paper | Key Finding | Validates/Challenges Our Hypothesis | ArXiv ID |
|-------|------------|-------------------------------------|----------|

### Cross-Paper Synthesis: N Actionable Principles
| # | Principle | Evidence | Application to Our Experiment |
|---|-----------|----------|-------------------------------|

### Gaps Identified
- [What hasn't been tried that we should explore]
- [What contradictions exist between sources]

### Recommendation
[Specific experiment design modifications informed by synthesis]
```

### 2.4 How It Applies to SKILL.md

The AI employee should:
- **🟢 Autonomous**: Search internal experiment logs (`~/.mlexp/experiments.jsonl`), Confluence pages, and ArXiv for relevant prior work
- **🟢 Autonomous**: Generate structured synthesis with evidence classification
- **🟡 Confirm**: Present synthesis and ask human to validate relevance and approve "actionable principles"
- **🔴 Human required**: Decision on whether literature supports proceeding vs. pivoting

**Source:** [Weekly ML Research Digest](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020d99ef610963a4c71971dd69cb6705ca0/pages/6889646944), [Literature Review Agent](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020da27561b9bb14249879a9abe6a41ad1c/pages/6698141159), [Academic Literature Review — 5 ArXiv Papers](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~111538891/pages/7035099887)

---

## 3. PARALLEL-ARCHITECTURE ORCHESTRATION

### 3.1 What It Is
A methodology for running5–10 model architecture variants simultaneously, managing compute resources, tracking configurations, and comparing results systematically.

### 3.2 How It Works — Internal Platform

**ML Studio Workflow Types** (from [Running Experiments in ML Studio](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CT5/pages/6850703587)):

| Type | Module PRGB | Customer Data | Compliance | Use Case |
|------|-------------|---------------|------------|----------|
| 🧪EXPERIMENTAL | ❌ Not required | 🚫 No | ⚠️ Not compliant | Quick prototyping |
| 🧭 ADHOC | ✅ Required | ✅ Yes | ⚠️ Not compliant | One-off experiments on prod data |
| 🚀 RELEASED | ✅ Required | ✅ Yes | 🟢 Compliant | Production workflows |

**Agentic ML Experiment Loop (`mlexp`)** (from [Agentic ML Experiment Loop](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020d2e2445580da4d86b9e6a5e346190367/pages/6722400136)):

Experiment Log Schema (each parallel run):
```json
{
  "id": "266c9d3f",
  "workflow_name": "...",
  "use_case": "...",
  "key_params": {
    "HF_MODEL_NAME": "...",
    "BEHAVIOR_WEIGHT": "...",
    "SEMANTIC_WEIGHT": "...",
    "TRAIN_DATASET": "..."
  },
  "output_tables": { "inference_test_table": "...", "eval_output_table": "..." },
  "metrics": null,
  "notes": "",
  "tags": []
}
```

CLI commands for orchestration:
- `mlexp log --yaml ...` — log experiment
- `mlexp list` — list all experiments  
- `mlexp diff<id1> <id2>` — compare two experiments
- `mlexp sync` — sync metrics from Databricks output tables
- `mlexp track` — track running experiments

**Forms of an ML Training Experiment** (from [The Forms of an ML Training Experiment](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/3229221104)):
> "An ML Practitioner may wish to run the same training workflow against20 different base model architectures. They need to flexibly exchange the model training component, but re-use the same data preparation and evaluation components."

### 3.3 Parallel Orchestration Pattern for5–10 Architectures

```
Step 1: Define shared components- Data preparation (fixed across all variants)
  - Evaluation module (fixed metrics, fixed test set)
  - Infrastructure constraints (GPU type, memory, timeout)

Step 2: Define variable components (per architecture)
  - Model architecture / HF model name
  - Hyperparameters (learning rate, batch size, etc.)
  - Feature set variation
  - Training schedule

Step 3: Launch parallel ADHOC workflows in ML Studio
  - Each variant gets unique experiment ID
  - Shared use_case for data access
  - Log to mlexp with key_params

Step 4: Monitor and early-stop
  - Track loss curves via ML Lens
  - Kill runs exceeding cost budget or diverging
  - Auto-sync metrics when complete

Step 5: Cross-variant comparison
  - mlexp diff for pairwise comparison
  - Streamlit eval tool for query-level drill-down
  - Generate leaderboard
```

### 3.4 How It Applies to SKILL.md

The AI employee should:
- **🟢 Autonomous**: Generate workflow YAML variants from a template with architecture-specific parameters
- **🟢 Autonomous**: Launch parallel ADHOC workflows, log experiments via `mlexp`
- **🟢 Autonomous**: Monitor runs, auto-sync metrics, flag diverging runs
- **🟡 Confirm**: Present early results and ask whether to kill underperforming variants (cost control)
- **🔴 Human required**: Approve compute budget for parallel runs before launch

**Source:** [Agentic ML Experiment Loop](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020d2e2445580da4d86b9e6a5e346190367/pages/6722400136), [The Forms of an ML Training Experiment](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/3229221104), [Running Experiments in ML Studio](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CT5/pages/6850703587), [Current ML End-to-End Experiment Landscape](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020e8e542e6fbb547dea43317e50413b60f/pages/6676884085)

---

## 4. ABLATION STANDARDS

### 4.1 What It Is
A rigorous methodology for removing components one at a time from a system to measure each component's individual contribution to overall performance.

### 4.2 How It Works — Internal Example (Gold Standard)

The **Search Metric Agent Ablation Study** ([Initial Evaluation to improve Search Metric Agent: Ablation Study Eval Review](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020f653a69aee43416088be29875748b11b/pages/6681791879)) provides a detailed internal template:

**Study Design:**

| Condition | Knowledge | Enforcement | SKILL.md | External Sources |
|-----------|-----------|-------------|----------|-----------------|
| Vanilla | ✅ |❌ | ❌ |✅ |
| A: Bare | ✅ | ❌ |✅ | ✅ |
| B: Protocol | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |
| C: No-enforce | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ |
| D: No-fallback | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ |
| Full System | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |✅ |

**Key Ablation Principles from this study:**
1. **One variable at a time** — each condition removes exactly one layer
2. **Replication** — Condition C had 3 runs to measure variance (σ≈0.87)
3. **Blind evaluation** — blind prompt tests that remove experimenter bias
4. **Layer attribution matrix** — quantify each component's marginal lift
5. **Causal claims review** — explicit verdicts (✅ WELL-SUPPORTED /🟡 DIRECTIONALLY CORRECT /❌ NOT SUPPORTED)
6. **Limitations stated** — "sufficient for internal directional evidence, not publication-grade causal claims"
7. **Scaling strategy derived** — prioritized investment based on marginal lift per layer

**Layer Attribution Matrix Template:**
| Layer | Metric1 | Metric2 | ... | Marginal Lift |
|-------|---------|---------|-----|---------------|
| Component A | Critical | Neutral | ... | +X.X |
| Component B | Required | Required | ... | Necessary but insufficient |

### 4.3 ML Craft Excellence Standard

From [ML Craft Excellence Signals](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~750924760/pages/5642415635):
> "Technical Decisions & Direction: Conducting impact analysis using data analysis and hypothesis testing... Offline evaluations, Ablation tests → HLDs and LLDs, POCs"

Ablation tests are listed as a **P40+ craft signal** demonstrating technical decision-making maturity.

### 4.4Ablation Study Workflow for ML Experiments

```
Step 1: Define Full System
  - Document all components (features, layers, preprocessing steps, etc.)
  - Establish baseline performance with full system

Step 2: Design Ablation Conditions
  - Remove ONE component per condition
  - Keep all other components identical
  - Include "vanilla/baseline" condition (minimal system)

Step 3: Execute with Replication
  - Minimum N≥3 runs per condition (for variance estimation)
  - Use identical random seeds where possible
  - Track compute cost per condition

Step 4: Analyze
  - Score each condition on all evaluation metrics
  - Compute marginal lift per component: Full - (Full minus component)
  - Build Layer Attribution Matrix
  - State causal claims with evidence classification

Step 5: Decision
  - Rank components by marginal lift
  - Identify "necessary but insufficient" vs "critical" components
  - Generate scaling/investment strategy
```

### 4.5 How It Applies to SKILL.md

The AI employee should:
- **🟢 Autonomous**: Generate ablation condition matrix from system component list
- **🟢 Autonomous**: Execute ablation runs (same infrastructure as parallel architecture)
- **🟢 Autonomous**: Compute layer attribution matrix and marginal lifts
- **🟡 Confirm**: Present causal claims with evidence classification for human review
- **🔴 Human required**: Approve "shipping decision" based on ablation results (which components to keep)

**Source:** [SMA Ablation Study](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020f653a69aee43416088be29875748b11b/pages/6681791879), [ML Craft Excellence Signals](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~750924760/pages/5642415635), Jira issue [ROVOSRP-1282: Ablation Study](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/ROVOSRP-1282)

