## 3. PARALLEL-ARCHITECTURE ORCHESTRATION

### 3.1 What It Is
A methodology for running5–10 model architecture variants simultaneously, managing compute resources, tracking configurations, and comparing results systematically.

### 3.2 How It Works — Internal Platform

**ML Studio Workflow Types** (from [Running Experiments in ML Studio](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CT5/pages/6850703587)):

| Type | Module PRGB | Customer Data | Compliance | Use Case |
|------|-------------|---------------|------------|----------|
| 🧪EXPERIMENTAL | ❌ Not required | 🚫 No | ⚠️ Not compliant | Quick prototyping |
| 🧭 ADHOC | ✅ Required | ✅ Yes | ⚠️ Not compliant | One-off experiments on prod data |
| 🚀 RELEASED | ✅ Required | ✅ Yes | 🟢 Compliant | Production workflows |

**Agentic ML Experiment Loop (`mlexp`)** (from [Agentic ML Experiment Loop](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020d2e2445580da4d86b9e6a5e346190367/pages/6722400136)):

Experiment Log Schema (each parallel run):
```json
{
  "id": "266c9d3f",
  "workflow_name": "...",
  "use_case": "...",
  "key_params": {
    "HF_MODEL_NAME": "...",
    "BEHAVIOR_WEIGHT": "...",
    "SEMANTIC_WEIGHT": "...",
    "TRAIN_DATASET": "..."
  },
  "output_tables": { "inference_test_table": "...", "eval_output_table": "..." },
  "metrics": null,
  "notes": "",
  "tags": []
}
```

CLI commands for orchestration:
- `mlexp log --yaml ...` — log experiment
- `mlexp list` — list all experiments  
- `mlexp diff<id1> <id2>` — compare two experiments
- `mlexp sync` — sync metrics from Databricks output tables
- `mlexp track` — track running experiments

**Forms of an ML Training Experiment** (from [The Forms of an ML Training Experiment](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/3229221104)):
> "An ML Practitioner may wish to run the same training workflow against20 different base model architectures. They need to flexibly exchange the model training component, but re-use the same data preparation and evaluation components."

### 3.3 Parallel Orchestration Pattern for5–10 Architectures

```
Step 1: Define shared components- Data preparation (fixed across all variants)
  - Evaluation module (fixed metrics, fixed test set)
  - Infrastructure constraints (GPU type, memory, timeout)

Step 2: Define variable components (per architecture)
  - Model architecture / HF model name
  - Hyperparameters (learning rate, batch size, etc.)
  - Feature set variation
  - Training schedule

Step 3: Launch parallel ADHOC workflows in ML Studio
  - Each variant gets unique experiment ID
  - Shared use_case for data access
  - Log to mlexp with key_params

Step 4: Monitor and early-stop
  - Track loss curves via ML Lens
  - Kill runs exceeding cost budget or diverging
  - Auto-sync metrics when complete

Step 5: Cross-variant comparison
  - mlexp diff for pairwise comparison
  - Streamlit eval tool for query-level drill-down
  - Generate leaderboard
```

### 3.4 How It Applies to SKILL.md

The AI employee should:
- **🟢 Autonomous**: Generate workflow YAML variants from a template with architecture-specific parameters
- **🟢 Autonomous**: Launch parallel ADHOC workflows, log experiments via `mlexp`
- **🟢 Autonomous**: Monitor runs, auto-sync metrics, flag diverging runs
- **🟡 Confirm**: Present early results and ask whether to kill underperforming variants (cost control)
- **🔴 Human required**: Approve compute budget for parallel runs before launch

**Source:** [Agentic ML Experiment Loop](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020d2e2445580da4d86b9e6a5e346190367/pages/6722400136), [The Forms of an ML Training Experiment](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/3229221104), [Running Experiments in ML Studio](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CT5/pages/6850703587), [Current ML End-to-End Experiment Landscape](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020e8e542e6fbb547dea43317e50413b60f/pages/6676884085)

