# Research Findings: Role-Doc §4 Runbooks/Eval Frameworks/Query Patterns — Classification for Skill/Tool Encoding

## 1. Primary Source Document: "Digital MLE: AI Employee Vision"

**Source**: [Digital MLE: AI Employee Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/7051610727/Digital+MLE+AI+Employee+Vision)

This page serves as the **de facto "role-doc §4"** for the Digital MLE AI Employee. It defines five core responsibilities with explicit autonomy tiers (🟢/🟡/🔴) and maps the full ML lifecycle into operational patterns. The page is authored by Ke Wang and is part of the AI Employee Strategy initiative.

###1.1 Core Responsibility Areas (from the Vision doc)

| **Responsibility** | **What the digital MLE owns** | **Autonomy Level** |
|---|---|---|
| Training Data Management | Data freshness monitoring, tenant list refresh, feature coverage reporting, training/serving skew detection | 🟢 Monitoring/refresh; 🟡 New sources; 🔴 Cross-team agreements |
| Model Training & Versioning | Scheduled retraining, artifact versioning, training run summaries, multi-version offline comparison, regression detection | 🟢 Scheduled runs; 🟡 Architecture changes; 🔴 Model design |
| Offline Evaluation | SxS eval suite execution, MRR/NDCG/precision reporting, win/loss extraction, regression gate | 🟢 Eval execution; 🟡 Regression interpretation; 🔴 Go/no-go decision |
| Online Experiment Monitoring | Statsig scorecard polling, metric summary, significance assessment, launch doc population | 🟢 Monitoring/docs; 🟡 Interpretation; 🔴 Launch/rollback |
| Production Health & Ramp | Serving latency/throughput monitoring, ramp schedule tracking, latency regression alerting | 🟢 Monitoring/alerting; 🟡 Ramp progression; 🔴 Incident response |

### 1.2 mini-mle Architecture (Implementation Foundation)

The implementation foundation is **mini-mle**, which has three components:
- **Skills**: Packaged prompts with ML + MLSE context (two layers: MLSE skills for setup/onboarding, MLE skills for experiments/evaluation)
- **Harnesses**: Connectors to ML runtime (ML Fabric interactive, ML Studio) — automate job submission, build monitoring, debugging loops via Bitbucket, Jira, ML Studio CLI
- **Agent Wiki**: Progressive disclosure knowledge base for navigating large codebases

---

## 2. Located Operational Artifacts

### 2.1 ML-Engine Circuit Breaker Runbook

**Source**: [ML-Engine Circuit Breaker Runbook](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ITSOL/pages/6058351626/ML-Engine+Circuit+Breaker+Runbook)

**What it is**: Production runbook documenting the circuit breaker (CB) for ML-Engine service. Optimized for on-call response.

**Key components**:
- Configuration Parameters (sliding window 60s, failure threshold 20%, min calls 20, half-open probes 20)
- Operational Playbooks:
  1. **CB Tripped (Open State)** — Validate alert in Splunk, inspect5xx errors, check downstream health, consider disabling CB via feature gate
  2. **Frequent CB Trips** — Correlate with traffic/deployments, review error taxonomy, evaluate thresholds
  3. **Tuning & Safe Changes** — Propose with rollback plan, canary rollout, evidence recording

**Classification**: **Both skill-encodable AND tool-automatable**
- *Skill-encodable*: The SOP-3 Production Incident Response skill can encode the decision tree (validate → inspect → check downstream → escalate/disable)
- *Tool-automatable*: Splunk query generation, Switcheroo feature gate toggle (via switcheroo MCP), Slack escalation cadence (via slack MCP), Jira incident ticket creation

### 2.2 End-to-End ML Framework @ Atlassian

**Source**: [End-to-End ML Framework @ Atlassian](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202046686c7c93274f379403a2283d58293f/pages/6708596091/End-to-End+ML+Framework+Atlassian)

**What it is**: Comprehensive documentation of Atlassian's "standard path" for ML models in FY25-26.

**Architecture pipeline (7 stages)**:
1. Data Lake & Feature Stores (Socrates tables, Tecton feature views)
2. Training & Experimentation (Databricks notebooks, MLflow)
3. Workflow Orchestration (ML Studio DAGs)
4. Model Registry & Serving Control Plane (MSP/Tarot v3, ML Registry, Deployment Store)
5. Serving Backends (SageMaker endpoints + TEAMServe Triton)
6. Product Services (Micros with SD.yml referencing MSP model groups)
7. Users and Clients

**Classification**: **Skill-encodable** (provides the conceptual framework for all nine skills to reference as the canonical pipeline stages)

### 2.3 Eval Framework for AI Development

**Source**: [Eval framework for AI development](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~63749bfc5fc160544e17adb9/pages/6480823539/Eval+framework+for+AI+development)

**What it is**: 8-step practical eval lifecycle framework used at Atlassian.

**Steps relevant to skill encoding**:
1. Run early vibe checks (20-50 prompts, label✓/~/✗)
2. Launch: Online Monitoring + Feedback Loops (collect traces, review30-50, categorize)
3. Decide what to fix vs evaluate (spec check → system check → generalisation check)
4. Build automated evaluation
5. Scale to complex agents (failure funnels, step-by-step funnel view)
6. Launch with online monitoring (instrument production, set guardrails, weekly review)

**Classification**: **Skill-encodable** (directly maps to SOP-2 evaluation steps and provides the eval candidate taxonomy for the eval-framework tool)

### 2.4 The New Eval Paradigm: Validation Stack + Eval Actor Stack

**Source**: [The New Eval Paradigm](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6884917799/The+New+Eval+Paradigm+From+Construction+to+Evaluation)

**What it is**: Two complementary frameworks for AI evaluation:
- **Validation Stack (Tiers1-3)**: Intent Fidelity → Architectural Cohesion → Non-Functional Guardrails
- **Eval Actor Stack (Levels 0-4)**: AI Self-Eval → Automated CI/CD → Engineer → Stakeholder → Production

**Classification**: **Skill-encodable** (provides the theoretical backbone for determining when the Digital MLE can self-evaluate vs. when to escalate)

### 2.5 RFC: Model Re-training using Data Quality and ML Lens

**Source**: [RFC: Model Re-training using Data Quality and ML Lens](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6013257858/RFC+Model+Re-training+using+Data+Quality+and+ML+Lens)

**What it is**: RFC for automated metric-driven model retraining workflow in ML Studio.

**Key patterns**:
- ML Lens emits drift/quality metrics to StreamHub
- ML Studio Orchestrator consumes metrics to determine retraining triggers
- Configurable user thresholds for re-training conditions
- Routine metric collection for data quality and drift

**Classification**: **Both skill-encodable AND tool-automatable**
- *Skill-encodable*: The SOP-1Drift-Triggered Retraining skill encodes the 9-step workflow (detect drift → validate → trigger → retrain → compare → gate → deploy)
- *Tool-automatable*: ML Lens metrics query (via serving-infra tool), StreamHub metric emission, ML Studio workflow trigger (via ML CI/CD tool)

### 2.6 Feature Drift Detection Architecture

**Source**: [Feature Drift Detection — Architecture Proposal](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/KYC/pages/6866709029/Feature+Drift+Detection+Architecture+Proposal)

**What it is**: Architecture proposal for feature drift detection using statistical tests.

**Key components**:
- Two-snapshot comparison (current vs. reference)
- Auto-detect statistical test from data properties (type, cardinality, sample size)
- Configurable reference dataset strategy
- Integration with ML Lens DataLoader and MetricsStore

**Classification**: **Tool-automatable** (drift detection calculations and threshold checks are API operations for the eval-framework tool)

