─── Response ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

*Schema (YAML/JSON, .sd.yml):

 • Canonical JSON Schema published at
   https://micros.prod.atl-paas.net/schema/service-descriptor.json (usable as
   $schema for editor LSP and AI-side pre-validation).
 • Authoritative reference: Confluence MICROS/167213240 (go/sdr).
 • Top-level blocks: description, organization, links
   (healthcheck/deepcheck/runOnce), source, compose, resources, buildNumber,
   compute.type (ec2 only — KubeCompute decommissioned),
   computeClassification, config.environmentVariables, deployment.type
   (cutover/hot-swap; progressive deprecated → use Spinnaker), loadBalancer
   (ALB default since 2019-07-19), network (internal-only ingress), scaling
   (instance/useInstanceWith/autoHibernation/schedule/alarms), requiresAsap,
   workers, serviceProxy, deploymentRestrictions, environmentOverrides.<env>,
   lifecycleEvents.timeout, oldStackLingerTime.
 • Templating: {{ resource service='…' type='…' name='…' property='…' }} for
   cross-service resource ARNs/URLs (sqs, sns-topic, elasticsearch, dynamodb,
   rds…).
 • Validation: atlas micros service validate -s -e -f (also run implicitly by
   deploy, local, resource set); render effective doc with -o yaml | yq ….

Auth model (today):

 • Two implicit resource roles: service owner (admin: transfer, delete prod,
   delete service) and everyone else on team/deploy-tokens (most ops; undeploy
   not blocked). Identity-based override for Micros sysadmins, kremlin,
   automation-engine, quickshard.
 • Backed by SSAM Access Container per service (Owner + Delegates + Access
   Levels of Users/Role Groups); Micros mirrors team membership to AD groups.
 • AuthN: Slauth (atlas slauth token -a micros-server --mfa, MFA required for
   writes) for users; ASAP audience micros-server for services (prod = 
   read/write, dev/staging = read-only); ephemeral build-server JWTs for CI
   (Bamboo / Bitbucket Pipelines / Spinnaker / Argo) via atlas micros service
   deploytoken add|list|delete (or atlas spin service deploytoken register);
   Service Proxy egress dependency on micros with
   X-Slauth-Egress/X-Slauth-Audience headers.
 • HTTP API base: https://micros.prod.apd-paas.net/api/v1/…; OpenAPI 2.0 at
   /swagger.json; rate limit 200 req/min/principal.
 • team transfer requires caller = current owner OR owner's direct manager;
   new owner must already be team add'd; LDAP sync ≤15 min; Microscope
   Observability Owner + OpsGenie team are separate out-of-band edits.
 • Granular per-env / per-op authorization is an open project (MEXT/Granular 
   Authorization for Service Management) — today the model is too coarse for
   unsupervised AI writes.

Coverage vs gaps for ML CI/CD tool (1B):

 • Covered today: read service doc, validate, render effective doc,
   register/list Spinnaker deploy tokens, ownership lookup. Bitbucket
   Pipelines must be handled via the existing Bitbucket MCP — don't
   reimplement.
 • Gaps the new tool must own: (1) safe descriptor edit primitive
   (key-order-preserving YAML patch + schema validate + PR), (2) permission
   preflight (resolve SSAM membership before write), (3) tool-layer per-env
   policy gate (since Micros doesn't enforce per-env yet), (4) unified
   Spinnaker deploy-token lifecycle (atlas micros … deploytoken + atlas spin 
   …), (5) drift detection for Microscope/OpsGenie out-of-band fields, (6)
   explicit validate / propose-PR / deploy separation (no silent chaining; no
   idempotent "apply" verb exists), (7) round-trip key-order stability (SHIPIT
   57 reordering caveat), (8) built-in 200 rpm back-off.

AI-employee guardrails: autonomous reads only; require explicit
human-in-the-loop confirmation for any write (deploy, undeploy, team transfer,
deploytoken add/delete, PR merges). 401 → --mfa Slauth re-prompt; healthcheck
failure → surface CFN events + MDEV runbook.


