# Research: Proactive Data-Quality Audit Standards for ML Data-Quality Audit Skill

## Executive Summary

This research identifies concrete patterns, frameworks, metrics, and workflows for building a proactive data-quality audit skill that an AI employee can execute autonomously or with human oversight. Findings are drawn primarily from Atlassian's internal ML platform tooling (ML Data Quality SDK, ML Lens, Feature Store quality pipelines), industry-standard frameworks (NIST AI RMF, ISO 42001/5259), and established open-source tools (Great Expectations, Evidently, dbt-expectations).

---

## 1. Data Quality Dimensions Taxonomy

### What it is
A structured classification of data quality concerns specific to ML training/inference data, organized into measurable dimensions.

### How it works
Based on internal Atlassian practices and industry standards, the canonical ML data quality dimensions are:

| Dimension | Definition | Key Metrics | ML Relevance |
|-----------|-----------|-------------|--------------|
| **Completeness (Coverage)** | Are all expected records/fields present? | Null %, row count vs expected, missing feature % | Incomplete features → model cannot learn patterns |
| **Accuracy (Fidelity)** | Do values match source of truth? | Rule-based validation pass rate, range checks | Wrong values → model learns incorrect patterns |
| **Timeliness (Freshness)** | Is data current enough for its purpose? | Max lag between latest record and current time, SLA compliance | Stale data → model makes decisions on outdated information |
| **Consistency** | Are values coherent across tables/time? | Cross-table reconciliation, metric comparison between pipeline stages | Inconsistencies → conflicting signals during training |
| **Uniqueness** | Are there duplicate records? | Primary key uniqueness, composite key validation | Duplicates → biased sampling in training |
| **Validity** | Are values within acceptable ranges/formats? | Range checks, regex validation, enum enforcement | Invalid values → noise or crashes during training |
| **Volume** | Is the quantity of data within expected bounds? | Day-over-day row count change, baseline comparisons | Volume drops → underfitting; spikes → potential data ingestion errors |
| **Schema Stability** | Has the structure of data changed? | Column add/drop/type-change detection | Schema changes → pipeline failures or silent feature corruption |

### How it applies to the skill
The skill should run checks across ALL dimensions in a single audit cycle, organized into a priority-ordered checklist. Each dimension maps to specific SDK calls and threshold evaluations.

### Source
- [Quality Assurance for Data Science](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ITSOL/pages/4106134264) — Atlassian internal
- [Data Quality Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DATA/pages/6540067714) — Atlassian Corporate DE
- [AI Data Quality Initiative](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DATA/pages/6364577392) — Atlassian internal
- NIST AI RMF / ISO 42001 Annex A.7.4 (Data Quality)

---

## 2. Distribution Shift / Drift Detection Standards

### What it is
Statistical methods to detect when the distribution of features or labels has changed between a reference period and the current period, which is a leading indicator of model performance degradation.

### How it works

#### Statistical Tests by Feature Type

| Feature Type | Statistical Test | Metric | Threshold Conventions |
|-------------|-----------------|--------|----------------------|
| **Continuous** | Population Stability Index (PSI) | Divergence between binned distributions | PSI < 0.1 = no drift; 0.1–0.25 = moderate; > 0.25 = significant |
| **Continuous** | Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) Test | Max CDF difference, p-value | p-value < 0.05 = significant drift |
| **Continuous** | Wasserstein Distance | Earth-mover distance | Threshold = 0.1 (Evidently default); magnitude-aware |
| **Categorical** | Chi-squared Test | Whether proportions changed | p-value < 0.05 = significant |
| **Categorical** | Jensen-Shannon Divergence (JSD) | Symmetric distribution divergence | 0 = identical, 1 = maximally different; threshold ~0.1 |
| **Categorical** | PSI (adapted) | Bin-frequency divergence | PSI >= 0.2 = drift detected (Atlassian internal threshold) |
| **Any** | KL-Divergence | Information-theoretic divergence | Asymmetric; useful for directional shift analysis |

#### Reference Window Strategies

| Window Type | Description | Use Case |
|-------------|-------------|----------|
| **Training baseline** | Distribution captured at model training time | Long-term structural drift detection |
| **Rolling N-day** (default28-30days) | Trailing period of production data | Short-term operational drift |
| **Fixed reference period** | User-specified date range | Controlled comparison (e.g., post-deployment) |
| **Same-period prior year** | Seasonal comparison | Seasonality-adjusted drift |

#### Atlassian's ML Lens Implementation
- **Drift Computation Job** runs daily via Cronman
- Computes KS Test (D-statistic, p-value) and PSI for all registered features
- Metrics emitted to `feature_observability.drift.*` namespace
- Alert evaluation is **opt-in** — feature owners register alert configs via MLFS SDK
- Default thresholds: KS p-value ≤ 0.05, KS-D ≥ 0.1, PSI threshold configurable

### How it applies to the skill
The audit skill should:
1. 🟢 **Autonomously** compute drift metrics for all monitored features using the ML DQ SDK
2. 🟢 **Autonomously** compare against reference distributions (training baseline + rolling window)
3. 🟡 **Escalate** when PSI > 0.25 or KS p-value < 0.01(severe drift) — recommend retraining
4. 🔴 **Human required** for changing reference distributions or adjusting thresholds

