## 8. Autonomy Tiers for Data Quality Actions

### What it is
Classification of data quality audit actions into 🟢/🟡/🔴 autonomy tiers aligned with Symphony3-tier autonomy.

### How it works

| Tier | Actions | Rationale |
|------|---------|-----------|
| **🟢 Autonomous** | Compute all metrics; generate profiles; detect drift; check freshness; create informational reports; post non-blocking alerts; update dashboards | Read-only analysis with no blast radius |
| **🟡 Confirm before executing** | Create P2Jira tickets; send P2Slack alerts; recommend retraining; flag features for deprecation review; publish audit reports externally | Moderate visibility/impact; human should review classification |
| **🔴 Human required** | Block a pipeline; update reference distributions/schema snapshots; change alert thresholds; deprecate features; trigger production retraining; modify data contracts | High impact; irreversible or policy-changing decisions |

### Key Principle from Internal Practice
> "Schema snapshot NEVER auto-updated — always a manual human action" — [Pipeline Overview for Data Quality](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120203449db23d7b349e8a784d08887b5bb22/pages/6695890115)

This principle generalizes: any action that changes the **definition of "normal"** (baselines, thresholds, schemas) requires human approval.

