                                 Key Findings

1. Directory layout — two valid shapes; recommend 1A for the three new tools:

 • Shape 1A (per-tool subdir with executor): tools/<tool_name>/{tool.json, 
   executor.py} — used by canonical role_setup outputs (calendar_team_events,
   calendar_ooo_scan). Pick this for serving-infra, ML CI/CD, eval-framework
   since they need fan-out, normalization, and partial-success handling.
 • Shape 1B (flat, no executor): tools/<tool_name>/tool.json only — used by
   _dev/sre_skills_tools/ when the tool is a thin CLI passthrough.

2. tool.json schema (converged across all 17+ examples examined):

 • Required: name, tool_type:"Action", category ("utility"|"workflow"),
   description (3–6 dense sentences stating wrapped MCP/CLI + gap closed +
   read/mutate posture), parameters[], returns (one-line JSON envelope).
 • Optional but recommended: executor (dotted import path pkg.mod:execute),
   examples[] (≥3), guardrails ({read_only, mutating, tier:1|2|3, 
   data_access_restrictions[]}), usage_guidance (markdown listing read-only /
   low-risk / high-risk buckets), aliases[], is_bridge, asynchronous,
   scenario.
 • Parameter object keys: name (use --double-dash flags), type
   (string|int|float|bool|flag|path|list[string]), required, positional,
   default, choices[], popular, description.

3. Return envelope convention (mandatory): JSON: { <request-echo>, partial: 
bool, <x>_gaps: [{id, error}], <payload>, ai_generated: true, fetched_at } —
never raise on sub-call failure; capture into the gap list and set partial: 
true.

4. executor.py template: File docstring (incl. explicit Authentication line) →
module-level constants (hard caps as named constants) → ISO-time helpers
(_parse_iso, _validate_window) → content sanitizers (_strip_*, _slim_*) →
ThreadPoolExecutor with bounded concurrency → single public execute(...)
returning the JSON envelope → _logger = logging.getLogger(__name__), never
print().

5. SKILL.md structure: YAML frontmatter (name, multi-line description trigger,
labels[], metadata.tools[]) → Overview → Leveraged Tools two-column markdown
table → numbered Workflow Mappings with Trigger + step-by-step CLI examples in
fences + Output template → Common patterns bash block → Prerequisites (auth
env vars) → Guidance nudges → Advanced → References. Split domain knowledge
into references/*.md chunked at <200 lines each (twg pattern with 11 files).

6. Guardrails — dual encoding: Combine structured guardrails block
(programmatic gating) with usage_guidance markdown buckets (Tier-1 autonomous
read / Tier-2 log+confirm mutation / Tier-3 explicit human approval).
Serving-infra rollback/canary = Tier 2–3; ML CI/CD
cancel-pipeline/descriptor-edit = Tier 2–3; eval-framework = Tier 1
throughout.

7. Integration patterns observed: (a) tool-composes-tool via executor import
(calendar_ooo_scan → calendar_team_events); (b) skill-routes-to-tool via
metadata.tools; (c) CLI-wrapper-for-MCP via single command positional with
auto-prefix (kubernetes_ops, cloud_ops); (d) bridge orchestrator
(is_bridge:true + asynchronous:true) — recommended for eval_runner multi-seed
runs.

8. Reference paths for synthesizers:

 • Canonical tool bundles:
   OpenStartup/test/openteam/.../20260419_234852/.../final_deliverables/tools/
   {calendar_team_events, calendar_ooo_scan}/
