Full report written to outputs/facet.md. The worker workspace had no local
artifacts (only inference logs), so the investigation is purely external
against authoritative Splunk developer docs, the Splunk Terraform provider
registry, Pulumi registry, signalfx GitHub repos, Claranet's pre-built module
library, and Google SRE / Splunk SLO guidance.

Key findings


  1 Three first-class authoring surfaces: the official
    splunk-terraform/signalfx provider (signalfx_detector resource — canonical
    IaC), Pulumi's signalfx.Detector (TS/Python/Go/C# mirror, v7.20 Dec 2025),
    and the signalfx-python REST/SignalFlow sub-clients (deprecated for
    ingest, still the only first-party Python path to the detector REST API).
    For sparse drift metrics, signalflow-client-python and Nike's
    signal_analog (declarative Python with dry-run) round out the SDK surface.
  2 Detector REST API is v2-only: POST/GET/PUT/DELETE /v2/detector, plus
    /validate, /{id}/disable|enable|incidents|events. v1 detectors created in
    the UI are invisible to the API — a real migration trap. Realm-prefixed
    endpoint (api.{realm}.signalfx.com), X-SF-Token header.
  3 SignalFlow detect lifecycle uses data(...) → transforms → 
    detect(when(predicate, lasting), off=when(...)).publish('label'). The
    signalfx.detectors.* built-in library (against_recent, against_periods,
    aperiodic, not_reporting, apm.*) supplies most common alert patterns;
    aperiodic.above_or_below_detector + lasting(window, fraction) is the right
    primitive for sparse drift signals.
  4 Define → list → update → fire mapping fully covered: preview via POST 
    /signalflow/preflight, validate via POST /detector/validate, plan via
    terraform plan/pulumi preview, apply, then poll /incidents and /events.
    Detector lifecycle is well-supported; the gap is global notification
    routing (must be encoded per-detector via rule.notifications).
  5 Cosine ≥ 0.60 floor encoding plan — first-class as schema parameters
    (cosine_floor=0.60, cosine_warn_at=0.75, cosine_recover=0.70,
    cosine_lasting=(30m, 0.8)), expanded into paired detect(when(cos < 0.60, 
    lasting('30m', 0.8)), off=when(cos > 0.70, 
    ...)).publish('embedding_drift_critical') plus a warn tier and a
    not_reporting heartbeat for the metric itself.
  6 Per-endpoint SLO YAML schema drafted covering latency_p95, error_rate,
    availability (compliance window), drift_cosine/ks/kl, data_quality, plus
    heartbeats and Google SRE multi-burn-rate pairs (14.4× over 1h+5m →
    Critical; 6× over 6h+30m → Major). Schema enforces severity→notification
    routing convention.
  7 Best practices to bake in: pair max_delay with extrapolation='last_value';
    always include runbook_url + tip; emit a not_reporting heartbeat for every
    business signal (Claranet pattern); use multi-burn-rate alerts (Splunk SLO
    docs implement Google's short=long/12 rule); author SignalFlow in UI first
    and extract; canonicalize program_text via templatefile() to avoid
    spurious diffs.
  8 AI autonomy guidance: read-ops, preview, validate, plan/preview, and
    threshold-only updates are autonomous; program_text changes,
    replace-triggering changes (detector_origin), deletes, and
    integration-token operations require human confirmation. Notification
    strings are positional comma-formatted with mandatory empty positions —
    the skill should expose typed objects internally and serialize only at the
    wire boundary.
  9 Cross-tool integration: drift metrics flow ML Lens → OTel → Splunk;
    detector runbook_url points at the ML-Engine Circuit Breaker Runbook in
    Confluence; tip carries the precise breaker-trip CLI; detector IaC lives
    in the responsible-ai-api Terraform with state segmentation (dashboards /
    detectors / admin-only integrations); Jira issues opened on create,
    mirrored on fire via Splunk's native Jira integration.
 10 Gaps: no global routing rule resource; v1 shadow detectors invisible to
    IaC; maxDelay capped at 900s (too short for hourly batch drift jobs — use
    aperiodic or change publish cadence); Splunk's own signalfx_slo resource
    has limited customization, so per-endpoint burn-rate alerting is best done
    by emitting two-window detect blocks manually following
    signalfx/terraform-signalfx-slx.
