                                 Key findings

Sources mined (Confluence): 14 pages, including the ML‑Engine Circuit Breaker
Runbook (ITSOL/6058351626), ML Lens RFC API spec (MLP/5626083662), ML Lens
Alerting Requirements (MLP/5989280688), Drift Detection design
(MLP/6738511349), DACI on drift compute (MLP/6858051319), Drift Detection
Metrics SDK (agd/6192530763), AlertSet CR (mkr/6247112824), SignalFx Detector
Best Practices (OBSERVABILITY/646560670), and the XPing Terraform module
(gai/6538429157). The responsible-ai-api repo is not present locally, so IaC
patterns are derived from the documented XPing module and AlertSet CR.

Circuit Breaker SLO conventions (canonical): 60s sliding window, minCalls=20,
20% failure threshold, 5xx only failure predicate, 30s→60s exponential backoff
in open state. These dictate per‑endpoint SLO invariants.

ML Lens API surface: Three core POST endpoints (/api/v1/run, /api/v1/metrics,
/api/v1/runs/metrics) plus POST /api/v1/alerts/register. Drift metrics live
under feature_observability.drift.{ks_d,ks_pvalue,psi} with standardised tags.
SDK classes: PopulationStabilityIndex, KLDivergence, KolmogorovSmirnov,
FeatureDrift (PySpark). Drift computation runs as a daily Databricks job (ML
Studio) that reads from StarRocks and emits via the Metrics API.

SignalFx detector authoring: Three blessed paths — (A) Terraform
signalfx_detector per XPing pattern with sfm plan/apply and Sauron prod
auto‑apply, (B) AtlasKube AlertSet CR (preferred for AtlasKube services) with
SignalFlow program + rules, (C) Python signalfx SDK fallback. Hard limits: 15k
detectors global, 20 detect()/det, 25 data()/det, 250k MTS/det.

Cosine ≥ 0.60 floor encoding plan: (1) ship new CosineSimilarity class in
atlassian_ml_dq_sdk.metrics.distribution (gap — currently absent), (2) emit to
feature_observability.drift.cosine namespace, (3) register an AlertConfig
preset with SDK guard rejecting values <0.60, (4) mirror in SignalFx via
detect(when(c<0.60, lasting='15m')). Justification: magnitude invariance for
embeddings, well‑defined in high dim, knee in same‑intent vs cross‑intent
cosine distribution.

Per‑endpoint SLO schema (net‑new): kind: EndpointSLO YAML with
windows{sliding,minCalls,lasting},
reliability{availability,latency.p50/p95/p99,throughput.minRps},
ml_quality{drift.{ks_pvalue,psi,cosine_floor},performance.{error_rate,timeout_
rate}}, notifications, tags. Compiler rules + invariants enforce 5xx‑only
failure codes, cosine ≥ 0.60, runbookUrl present, group‑by‑variant under one
detector.

Capability matrices & API contracts drafted for MLLensClient (10 methods incl.
define_cosine_floor) and SfxDetectorTool (12 methods covering compile → render
→ validate → plan → apply → list → fire). Both align to a unified
define→list→update→fire lifecycle so a single YAML drives both tools.

Gaps identified: (1) no CosineSimilarity in DQ SDK, (2) no first‑class
cosine_floor preset, (3) no documented update/delete on ML Lens alerts, (4) no
PATCH on SignalFx detectors, (5) heuristic guards not native, (6) Tecton not
first‑class in ML Lens, (7) per‑endpoint SLO compiler doesn't exist today, (8)
responsible-ai-api repo not in workspace.

AI autonomy guidance: All reads/queries/validate/plan/local codegen are
autonomous; all prod writes (PR creation, register/update/delete_alert, mute,
apply) require user confirmation. Cosine floor lowering attempts
auto‑rejected.

